# .gitignore for an AI-DLC reference deployment: Terraform, a container image,
# and a GitHub Actions workflow, run through AI-DLC v1 in Claude Code.
#
# This repository is a handover artifact, so the comments explain WHY rather than
# just WHAT. Two things are deliberately NOT ignored; see the end of the file.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Terraform state. The important one.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# State records every attribute of every resource, including values marked
# sensitive, in PLAINTEXT. Committing it once puts it in history permanently.
# This project keeps state in S3 instead, but a local apply or a failed init can
# still drop these files here.
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
*.tfstate.backup
terraform.tfstate.d/

# Crash logs can contain the same state values.
crash.log
crash.*.log

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Terraform local inputs and working files
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Variable files carry account ids, bucket names and role ARNs, and are where a
# secret ends up when someone is in a hurry. The example file IS tracked, because
# a reader reproducing this needs to know which variables to supply.
*.tfvars
*.tfvars.json
!example.tfvars
!*.tfvars.example

# Partial backend configuration. The state bucket name contains the AWS account
# id and this repository is public, so the backend is configured out of band with
# `terraform init -backend-config=backend.hcl` rather than in a tracked file.
backend.hcl
*.backend.hcl
!backend.hcl.example

# Provider binaries and module cache. Large, platform-specific, re-fetchable.
.terraform/

# Saved plans are binary, can embed sensitive values, and are only valid against
# the state they were generated from.
*.tfplan
tfplan
plan.out

# Local CLI config and local overrides. These silently change what applies, so a
# committed one would mean the repo does something different on your machine than
# on anyone else's.
.terraformrc
terraform.rc
override.tf
override.tf.json
*_override.tf
*_override.tf.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Credentials and secrets
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Nothing here should ever be needed: the deployment authenticates through
# federation, with no long-lived key to store. These entries exist to catch the
# accident, not to support a workflow.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
credentials
.aws/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Claude Code
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local-only settings, per the AI-DLC project's own version control guidance.
# Note CLAUDE.md and .aidlc-rule-details/ are tracked; see the end of this file.
.claude/settings.local.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# One operator's concrete values
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# docs/prerequisites.md is a template using <ACCOUNT_ID> placeholders so a reader
# can follow it with their own account. The filled-in version stays local.
# Account ids are not secrets, but this repository is handed to a customer and a
# partner, and neither needs to learn which account the demonstration ran in.
local-values.md

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cross-platform editor and OS noise
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Included although this is built on Linux, because the repository is handed to
# others who may be on macOS or Windows.
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp
*~
.idea/
.vscode/

# GitHub CLI writes a device identifier under here when its state directory
# resolves relative to the working tree. Local tool artifact, not project content.
.local/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT ignored, on purpose
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# aidlc-docs/
#   AI-DLC's own output: the stage artifacts, aidlc-state.md, and audit.md. This
#   is the record of which human approved which stage, and it is the evidence
#   that the process actually ran. Ignoring it would delete the point of the
#   exercise. It is committed.
#
# CLAUDE.md and .aidlc-rule-details/
#   The AI-DLC v1 rules themselves. Committed so the workflow travels with the
#   repository and anyone cloning it gets the same behaviour, which is what the
#   AI-DLC project recommends.
#
# .terraform.lock.hcl
#   The provider dependency lock. Committed deliberately: without it, a later
#   clone resolves different provider versions and can produce a different
#   result from the same code.
#
# Language-specific build artifacts
#   Not listed yet because the application's language is chosen during the
#   AI-DLC run, not before it. Add them once the stack exists, rather than
#   pasting in ignores for languages this project does not use.
